---
/* No taper schedules, no dose guidance, no "reduce gradually by" instructions.
   Every answer routes to supervision. This page will be found by people
   searching how to stop on their own. */
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can you die from benzodiazepine withdrawal?', a: "It is possible, yes — stopping abruptly after sustained use can cause seizures. Alongside alcohol it is one of only two substances where that is genuinely true. This is not said to frighten you into calling; it is said because a lot of people assume the worst case is feeling unwell for a week, and with these it is not." },
  { q: 'Can I taper myself at home?', a: "We would strongly encourage you not to, and we are not going to publish a schedule for doing it. The dose, the drug and how long you have been taking it all change what is safe, which is precisely why it is done with a physician. Call and we will talk through what a supervised version looks like." },
  { q: 'I was prescribed these. Does that change anything?', a: "It changes nothing about how you will be treated, and it is the most common way people arrive at this. Taking a medication as directed and finding you cannot stop is not a moral failure — it is a known property of the drug." },
  { q: 'Will I be taken off them immediately?', a: "No. These are reduced gradually under medical supervision rather than withdrawn suddenly, and your clinical team decides the pace based on your history and how you are doing." },
  { q: 'What about the anxiety they were treating?', a: "Treated at the same time, which is the point of doing this somewhere clinical. Our medical team reviews your medication, and the therapy addresses what the prescription was managing — because taking the drug away without that rarely holds." },
  { q: "I'm drinking as well as taking them.", a: "Then tell us that first, before anything else. It is the highest-risk combination we see and it directly changes the medical support prepared for your arrival. It is also common, and it is not something anyone will react to badly." },
  { q: 'How long does the detox take?', a: "Longer than most — benzodiazepines come down gradually rather than clearing in a few days, and the tail can extend beyond the detox stay itself. Your team will be straight with you about what they are seeing rather than committing to a number on day one." },
  { q: 'I only take a small dose.', a: "Then a call costs you nothing and settles it. Dose is only one of several things that matter here, and it is a reasonable question to ask someone clinical rather than to decide alone." },
];
---
<section id="benzo-faq" class="bg-cream py-[70px]">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <p class="text-gold font-body text-xs font-bold tracking-[0.18em] uppercase mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
      <span class="w-6 h-px bg-gold inline-block"></span>
      Straight answers
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-display text-charcoal mb-10">Questions about benzodiazepines</h2>
    <div class="divide-y divide-cream-soft border-t border-b border-cream-soft">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="group py-5">
          <summary class="flex items-start justify-between gap-6 cursor-pointer">
            <h3 class="font-body font-semibold text-charcoal text-[18px] leading-[28px]">{f.q}</h3>
            <span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-teal">
              <svg class="faq-plus w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg>
              <svg class="faq-minus w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M5 12h14"/></svg>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="font-body text-charcoal text-[16px] leading-[27px] mt-4 pr-10">{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="mt-10 bg-white border-l-2 border-gold rounded-r-xl p-6">
      <p class="font-body text-charcoal text-[16px] leading-[27px]">
        <strong class="font-bold">If you have already cut down and feel unwell,</strong> call
        <a href="tel:8883625576" class="text-teal font-semibold hover:opacity-70 transition-opacity tel-number">(888) 362-5576</a>
        or go to an emergency room today. Confusion, hallucinations or a seizure during benzodiazepine withdrawal need medical attention immediately.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
